WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.240
Face-carving killers! Whale carcasses!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.815
Pod people from beyond the stars! Keep watching&nbsp;
for more near-perfect horror movies… if you dare.

00:00:10.815 --> 00:00:16.320
Based on Jean Redon's novel of the same name,&nbsp;
Eyes Without a Face follows Dr. Génessier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.080
a plastic surgeon who feels responsible&nbsp;
for the role he played in the accident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.640
that disfigured his daughter, Christine. With&nbsp;
the help of his laboratory assistant Louise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.440
Dr. Génessier's desperation drives him to&nbsp;
abduct and peel the faces off young women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.640
so he can relieve his infantilized daughter&nbsp;
and her creepy, featureless plastic mask.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.200
According to Criterion, when the film&nbsp;
premiered in France the audience "dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.240
like flies" — especially during the infamously&nbsp;
graphic surgical scene. But the film enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.160
an enthusiastic critical re-evaluation&nbsp;
during its 1986 theatrical re-release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.760
and has since claimed a spot in&nbsp;
cinema history as a masterful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.120
and even poetic, piece of body horror&nbsp;
cinema. Slate's David Edelstein writes:

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.200
"Eyes Without A Face [is] among the&nbsp;
most disturbing horror films ever made."

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.760
Eyes Without a Face enjoys an average&nbsp;
98% fresh rating on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:15.160
and came in at number 38 in Time Out's list of the&nbsp;
100 best horror films. It holds a Metascore of 90.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.880
Co-directed by Merian C. Cooper and Ernest B.&nbsp;
Schoedsack, King Kong follows an adventurous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.000
film crew that discovers the "eighth wonder of&nbsp;
the world": a giant prehistoric ape who falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.600
head over heels for the beautiful Ann Darrow.&nbsp;
Kong's romantic aspirations are foiled, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.880
when the crew abduct him to New York City, where&nbsp;
the monstrous monkey promptly wreaks havoc.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.720
Sporting a weighted average Metascore of 90&nbsp;
and a 99% approval rating on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.920
Kong was a technical marvel for its time, boasting&nbsp;
groundbreaking technology such as stop-motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.880
animation and matte paintings. As Roger&nbsp;
Ebert puts it in his "Great Movies" write-up:

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.240
"In the very artificiality of&nbsp;
some of the special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.520
there is a creepiness that isn't there in&nbsp;
today's slick, flawless, computer-aided images."

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.320
Indeed, there's something unnervingly&nbsp;
tactile about Kong's stuttering movements;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.720
something primeval and ancient that&nbsp;
rhymes nicely with the film's mystique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.080
as one of the true milestones&nbsp;
of Hollywood movie-making.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.405
"It wasn't the airplanes.&nbsp;
'Twas beauty killed the beast."

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.000
Sitting pretty with a 90 Metacritic&nbsp;
score, Alfred Hitchcock's The Birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.760
did for birds what Jaws would&nbsp;
do for sharks a decade later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.040
A sub-genre-defining creature feature,&nbsp;
the film's swarms of avian adversaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.400
aren't so much individual animals as they&nbsp;
are a force of nature; a furious storm of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.640
beating wings and pecking beaks with unknown&nbsp;
motivations but a clear, murderous, objective.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.280
When the film first premiered in&nbsp;
1963, it was met with mixed reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.800
rejected, in part, for its overt allegiance&nbsp;
to the lowly trappings of exploitation cinema.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.600
And yet the passage of time won out, and&nbsp;
the critical tide turned in Hitch's favor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.160
The Birds now holds the number seven spot&nbsp;
on the American Film Institute's list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.760
of the greatest American thrillers. All told,&nbsp;
Hitchcock's movie remains a creepy, high-flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.833
benchmark when it comes to killer animal flicks.&nbsp;
Here's to never going outside ever again.

00:03:16.194 --> 00:03:20.480
A controversial, gothic tale&nbsp;
of tragedy and psychosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.680
disgraced director Roman Polanski's first&nbsp;
English-language film is widely praised as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.560
one of cinema's most unsettling portraits&nbsp;
of mental and emotional disintegration.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.760
A fragile young Belgian manicurist named Carol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.160
suffers from a debilitating fear of men.&nbsp;
When her sister and roommate Helen leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.360
their shabby apartment for an Italian getaway&nbsp;
with a married man, Carol retreats deeper into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.440
her own fractured mind, where visitations have&nbsp;
feverish and ultimately violent consequences.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.120
Following its premiere at the&nbsp;
1965 Cannes Film Festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
Repulsion was met with sizable critical acclaim&nbsp;
as "a small but piercing human tragedy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.480
per a 1965 review in The New York Times. The&nbsp;
film enjoys a near-perfect 95% approval rating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.880
on Rotten Tomatoes and clocks in at number 54 on&nbsp;
Time Out's list of the 100 best horror movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.320
An impressive sophomore effort that will surely&nbsp;
intrigue fans of claustrophobic terror trips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.355
Repulsion is a classic chiller well&nbsp;
deserving of its 91 Metacritic score.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:28.480
Don't let Frankenstein's age fool you. James&nbsp;
Whale's 1931 adaptation of Mary Shelley's novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.640
packs an enormous, harrowing punch. Boasting a&nbsp;
legendary and alarmingly sympathetic performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.880
by Boris Karloff as the Monster, Frankenstein&nbsp;
tells of an obsessive doctor driven to restore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.400
life to a creature assembled&nbsp;
out of scraps of rotting flesh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.720
While Dr. Frankenstein's experiment proves&nbsp;
successful, the piecemeal creature is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.680
confused, traumatized, and unable to move&nbsp;
peacefully through the world of the living.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.560
Ingrained in the pop culture consciousness and&nbsp;
saddled with the burden of historical relevance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.440
it's tempting to hold Frankenstein at arm's&nbsp;
length. But its reputation as a masterpiece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.880
is fully deserved. Frankenstein holds a&nbsp;
100% "Fresh" rating on Rotten Tomatoes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.240
frequently places on both genre-specific lists,&nbsp;
such as Bravo's "100 Scariest Movie Moments"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.920
and broader gauntlets like The New York&nbsp;
Times' Best 1000 Movies Ever. The American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.960
Film Institute ranked this line as the 49th&nbsp;
greatest movie quote in American cinema:

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.800
"It's alive, it's alive! It's alive, it's alive!"

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.438
The film claims an outstanding Metascore of 91.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:39.280
Son of Saul takes place almost entirely&nbsp;
in Auschwitz-Birkenau, a complex of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.200
concentration and extermination camps operated&nbsp;
by Nazi Germany during World War II. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:51.040
a Jewish-Hungarian prisoner named Saul serves as&nbsp;
a member of a unit of Jewish inmates forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.480
assist in the disposal of gas chamber victims.&nbsp;
The plot follows Saul's dangerous and desperate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.560
attempt to provide a proper Jewish burial for&nbsp;
a young boy he adopts posthumously as his son.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.120
As Christy Lemire astutely notes in her review&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.880
for RogerEbert.com, Son of Saul tackles&nbsp;
the ungraspable horrors of the Holocaust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.880
by placing its horrific elements&nbsp;
in the periphery, to the end that:

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.120
"The suggestion of suffering is more&nbsp;
unsettling than wallowing in it."

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:23.120
Likewise, The Film Stage's Giovanni Marchini Camia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:28.400
lauds the film as "a towering landmark for&nbsp;
filmic fictionalizations of the Holocaust."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.040
When it debuted at Cannes in 2015,&nbsp;
the film was awarded the Grand Prix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.960
and the prestigious FIPRESCI Prize. Later, at&nbsp;
the 88th Academy Awards, the film won the Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:45.524
for Best Foreign Language Film. On Metacritic,&nbsp;
Son of Saul holds an impressive score of 91.

00:06:45.524 --> 00:06:49.760
Even without directly invoking the&nbsp;
obvious trappings of the genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:53.520
Hungarian directors Béla Tarr and Ágnes Hranitzky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.160
expertly craft an atmosphere that is&nbsp;
unmistakably, and oppressively, horrific.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:04.480
Werckmeister Harmonies takes place in an anonymous&nbsp;
provincial town in the dead of winter. Despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.360
biting cold, townspeople gather to gape at&nbsp;
the main attraction of the traveling circus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.240
that now occupies the town square: the&nbsp;
massive, stuffed carcass of a whale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:19.680
People come from far and wide to gape at the&nbsp;
bloated monster, but, over time, the tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.960
between the townsfolk and the gawking visitors&nbsp;
becomes unbearable and erupts into violence.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.640
Like much of Tarr's work, Werckmeister Harmonies&nbsp;
is the dictionary definition of "slow cinema,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:36.240
composed of thirty-nine long, dreamlike&nbsp;
single-camera shots that play out languidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:42.480
over the course of the film's 145-minute runtime.&nbsp;
When the film was released in 2000, it was met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.520
with critical acclaim and praise for, as The&nbsp;
Guardian's Richard Williams puts it: a "bleak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.880
vision of chaos and capitalism." According to a&nbsp;
statistical breakdown from They Shoot Pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.960
Don't They, the film is presently the 34th most&nbsp;
critically acclaimed film of the 21st Century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.567
Unsurprisingly, it boasts&nbsp;
a 92 score on Metacritic.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.600
In a chilling blend of science fiction and horror,&nbsp;
Don Siegel's 1956 film Invasion of the Body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:15.760
Snatchers is one of the most astute political&nbsp;
allegories of 1950s America. Released at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:21.760
height of the cold war, the film's acute vision&nbsp;
of insidious conformity struck an anti-communist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.480
nerve while flirting with a more seditious&nbsp;
critique of the tyranny of the McCarthy era.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.820
"Can't you see, they're after you!&nbsp;
They're after all of us! Our wives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:35.280
our children, everyone! They're here already!"

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.560
Based on Jack Finney's serialized&nbsp;
1954 sci-fi novel, The Body Snatchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.600
the film follows a small-town doctor as he&nbsp;
slowly uncovers the truth about the recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.800
unsettling changes in his sleepy community.&nbsp;
Namely: the town is quietly being invaded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:56.400
an alien presence who gestate in massive seeds&nbsp;
and take over people's bodies as they sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.880
producing emotionless, indistinguishable&nbsp;
carbon copies of the original person.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:08.080
Boasting a 92 Metascore and an impressive 98%&nbsp;
approval rating on Rotten Tomatoes, in 2008&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:12.480
the American Film Institute recognized&nbsp;
the film as the ninth best entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.680
in the science-fiction genre. Steeped&nbsp;
in unchecked paranoia and alienation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.000
Invasion of the Body Snatchers is a taut&nbsp;
and atmospherically tense horror film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:28.125
with very little horror on-screen. All the better&nbsp;
to wreak horrific havoc on your imagination.

00:09:28.125 --> 00:09:32.800
Adapted from Daphne Du Maurier's&nbsp;
short story of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:39.120
1973's Don't Look Now is a trauma-laced&nbsp;
slow dive that feels, unavoidably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:43.600
like the logical extension of family&nbsp;
grief. It is a protracted nightmare that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.000
muddles the line between a psychological&nbsp;
break and the genuinely supernatural.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.680
The movie follows a married couple who move&nbsp;
to Venice after the death of their young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.560
daughter Christine. Confined in the city's&nbsp;
elegant decay, they both begin to experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:02.000
a series of terrifying and increasingly&nbsp;
dangerous occurrences that defy explanation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.760
Tempted by the irrational notion that&nbsp;
their daughter might still be alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.640
the couple form a disturbing friendship with two&nbsp;
sisters, one of whom claims to be clairvoyant.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:16.640
Directed by Nicolas Roeg, a former cinematographer&nbsp;
with an undeniable eye for color and composition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.640
Don't Look Now is a breathtaking marriage of&nbsp;
giallo tropes and pulpy intrigue. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:27.280
also features one of the most memorable&nbsp;
final scenes in genre film, a remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.680
climax that cements the film's reputation as&nbsp;
an all-time great. With a Metascore of 95,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.680
Roeg's stylish examination of the maddening power&nbsp;
of loss is essential viewing for genre fans.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.000
"We belong dead!"

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:47.520
Those three immortal words encapsulate the&nbsp;
macabre heartache of Bride of Frankenstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.520
James Whale's crushing follow-up&nbsp;
to his iconic gothic monster flick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.000
Amazingly, Bride is one of those rarities in&nbsp;
cinema: a sequel that is better than the original.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.320
Picking up right where the first film left&nbsp;
off, Bride of Frankenstein reconnects with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:06.800
the deranged doctor, who has fallen back&nbsp;
under the spell of his former mentor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:11.680
Dr. Pretorius. When Pretorius encourages&nbsp;
his pupil to resume his experiments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:16.640
and create a mate for the Monster, the shattered&nbsp;
Frankenstein finds that he cannot refuse.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.680
Frequently identified as the crown&nbsp;
jewel of Universal monster movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.720
Bride of Frankenstein's overarching theme about&nbsp;
a relationship society doesn't deem natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:31.520
retains its relevance to this day. And it's&nbsp;
all the more heart-breaking considering Whale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:38.400
himself was openly gay. With a fantastic 95 on&nbsp;
Metacritic, Bride of Frankenstein is a must-watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.184
for anyone looking to see what all the fuss is&nbsp;
about when it comes to classic-era horror films.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:50.000
In 1967, a horror novel graced the annual&nbsp;
best-sellers list of Publishers Weekly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.280
for the first time in 30 years: Rosemary's Baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:59.280
by Ira Levin. A precision thriller about the&nbsp;
devil impregnating a woman on the Upper West Side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:04.320
Levin's novel was swiftly adapted into a&nbsp;
film just one year after its publication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:10.000
Starring Mia Farrow as the titular Rosemary&nbsp;
and John Cassavetes as her husband Guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.680
Roman Polanski's satanic slow-burn frequently&nbsp;
tops "best of" lists for good reason.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:21.520
In 2014, Rosemary's Baby was selected by the&nbsp;
Library of Congress for preservation in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:27.520
National Film Registry. And in 2010, The Guardian&nbsp;
ranked it as the second-greatest horror film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.560
of all time. Ruth Gordon's performance&nbsp;
as Minnie Castevet, the nosy neighbor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:36.880
quite literally from hell, earned her an&nbsp;
Academy Award for best supporting actress;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.880
it would be the last Oscar awarded to a horror&nbsp;
movie until 1991's The Silence of the Lambs.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:47.920
Every film ambling under the umbrella&nbsp;
of "elevated horror" owes an immense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.880
demonic debt to Rosemary's Baby and its&nbsp;
revolutionary vision of satanists as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.680
ordinary folks who might pass you in the&nbsp;
street. With uncomfortably long takes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:02.720
claustrophobic set design, and immersive&nbsp;
trance-like pacing, this is a film that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:08.662
wheels and deals in pure psychological horror.&nbsp;
It's more than worthy of its 96 Metascore.

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:13.440
Psycho begins with a deceptively&nbsp;
conventional set-up: Marion Crane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.080
a real estate clerk, embezzles a sizable&nbsp;
chunk of change from her employer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:22.000
in the hope of starting a new life. When&nbsp;
she's caught in a torrential downpour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:27.120
she takes refuge at the Bates Motel, an&nbsp;
isolated establishment run by Norman Bates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:31.840
a quirky mama's boy who probably&nbsp;
wouldn't even hurt a fly. Probably.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:33.840
"We all go a little mad sometimes,"

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.520
Transitioning from a straight-forward&nbsp;
noir to something far nastier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:47.040
Psycho's unsettling portrait of a predatory&nbsp;
Peeping Tom remains as forceful today as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:54.080
was in 1960. While the film's devious rug pulls&nbsp;
are now widely known, Psycho continues to function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:59.360
as one of the most gripping and suggestive&nbsp;
entries in the horror-thriller sub-genre.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:03.120
With a leering camera that often&nbsp;
mirrors its predatory subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:08.080
the movie actively revels in the sinful,&nbsp;
voyeuristic allure of cinema itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:13.440
twisting the act of watching into something&nbsp;
deviant and transgressive. It makes perfect sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:19.616
then, that Alfred Hitchcock's enduring masterwork&nbsp;
boasts a near-flawless 97 on Metacritic.

